%I A399620 #29 Nov 21 2026 12:32:53
%S A399620 0,0,0,0,2,6,21,40,80,130,220,324,486,672,945,1248
%N A399620 Minimum number of monochromatic axis-parallel rectangles in a 2-coloring of an n X n grid.
%C A399620 A rectangle is determined by two distinct rows and two distinct columns; its four corners, not necessarily adjacent, must all have the same color.
%H A399620 Jeremy F. Alm and Alex Miller, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.19635">Constructive and computational methods for extremal and near-extremal box colorings</a>, arXiv:2611.19635 [math.CO], 2026. See p. 3.
%H A399620 Petr Goncharov, <a href="/A399620/a399620.txt">Matrices attaining a(9)-a(16) and lower bounds</a>.
%F A399620 a(4t-1) = (4t-1)*(2t-1)*(t-1)^2 whenever a Hadamard matrix of order 4t exists. Proof: two rows agreeing in m columns span at least floor((m-1)^2/4) monochromatic rectangles. Each column contains at least C(2t-1,2)+C(2t,2) = (2t-1)^2 pairs of equal entries, so the (4t-1)*(2t-1) row pairs agree in at least 2t-1 columns on average. Since floor((m-1)^2/4) is convex and nondecreasing, a(4t-1) >= (4t-1)*(2t-1)*(t-1)^2. Equality holds for the incidence matrix of a Hadamard 2-(4t-1,2t-1,t-1) design, e.g., the Paley matrix when 4t-1 is a prime power. - _Petr Goncharov_, Oct 18 2026
%e A399620 For n=5, the matrix with rows
%e A399620   00011,
%e A399620   00110,
%e A399620   01001,
%e A399620   11010,
%e A399620   10101
%e A399620 has exactly two monochromatic rectangles. They use rows 1,2 and columns 1,2, and rows 1,3 and columns 1,3. No fewer rectangles is possible, so a(5) = 2.
%e A399620 An 8 X 8 matrix attaining a(8) = 40 has rows
%e A399620   00001111,
%e A399620   00110011,
%e A399620   01011100,
%e A399620   01100101,
%e A399620   10101010,
%e A399620   10110100,
%e A399620   11001001,
%e A399620   11010010.
%Y A399620 Main diagonal of A399621. Cf. A191873, A191965.
